==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*mey- (change)}}
From {{uder|en|la|praetermitto}}.

===Pronunciation===
*{{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/pɹiːtəˈmɪt/}}
*{{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˌpɹidəɹˈmɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-pretermit.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|++}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To intentionally [[disregard]] (something), to [[ignore]]; to [[neglect]] or [[omit]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Donne Works|volume=IV|chapter=Sermon LXXXII|page=20|passage=[F]or the public, for the redemption of the whole world, God hath (shall we say, '''pretermitted'''?) derelicted, forsaken, abandoned, his own, and only Son.}}
#* {{RQ:Hobbes Leviathan|chapter=XXI|passage=The liberty of a subject lieth, therefore, only in those things which, in regulating their actions, the sovereign hath '''praetermitted''' (such as is the liberty to buy, and sell, and otherwise contract with one another; to choose their own abode, their own diet, their own trade of life, and institute their children as they themselves think fit; and the like).}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=c. 1598|author=w:Francis Bacon|title=An Account of {{...}}Compositions for Alienations|passage=The fees, or allowances, that are termly given to these deputies, receiver, and clerks, for recompence of these their pains, I do purposely '''pretermit'''; because they be not certain, but arbitrary.}}
#* {{RQ:Eliot Middlemarch|volume=I|book=II|chapter=XX|passage=[V]arious subjects for annotation have presented themselves which, though I have no direct need of them, I could not '''pretermit'''.}}

